--TEST--
Core\Crypto\PublicKey tells a key that does not check from a call that cannot be met: every unreadable key is one RuntimeError sentence, and an encoding a kind does not have or a private key handed over as a public one is a LogicError — `rule:core-classes/crypto-interop-tier`, `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Two refusals with two different readers. A key off the wire that does not
// check is a verdict on whoever sent it, so it is a `RuntimeError` and carries
// one sentence for every way of failing — a reason would tell a forger which
// half of the key landed. A call naming an encoding its kind does not have, or
// handing over a private key where a public one goes, is a mistake in the
// program and is a `LogicError`.
string $rsaSpki = "";

// `Raw` is the key material with nothing around it, and an RSA key has no such
// form. The refusal is at the call rather than at the octets, so it is the same
// refusal whether the octets were a key or not.
try {
    Core\Crypto\PublicKey $noRawForm = Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(
        Core\Encoding::fromHex($rsaSpki),
        Core\Crypto\KeyKind::RsaPkcs1,
        Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Raw
    );
    echo "raw rsa accepted", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}

// A JWK carrying `d` is a private key, and a program that hands one where a
// public key goes has made a mistake this member will not paper over by
// reading the public members out of it.
try {
    Core\Crypto\PublicKey $privateHalf = Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(
        "{\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"d\":\"Nue-RPHfwV1kqA-Y2PLXn5eIB6fEYmFy87eV3H4RBIo\",\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"x\":\"_M6QCKM1ZSSFYSlafOHMywQLYSm7qP8kdElTu8ASYaw\"}" as bytes,
        Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519,
        Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Jwk
    );
    echo "a private jwk accepted", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}

// The same reading one member along: a key that read cleanly still has no
// `Raw` spelling to write, and asking for one is the caller's bug and not the
// key's.
Core\Crypto\PublicKey $rsa = Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(
    Core\Encoding::fromHex($rsaSpki),
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::RsaPkcs1,
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Spki
);
try {
    bytes $nothingToWrite = $rsa->write(Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Raw);
    echo "raw rsa written", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}
if ($rsa->kind() == Core\Crypto\KeyKind::RsaPkcs1) {
    echo "a 2048-bit modulus is inside the roster", "\n";
}

// Every way of not being a key, asserted by counting and by counting the
// *distinct* sentences: a member that explained which check failed would still
// refuse all six and would fail the second total, which is the one that holds
// the refusal to carrying no oracle.
array<bytes> $unreadable = [
    // A P-256 point with its final octet flipped, which is off the curve.
    Core\Encoding::fromHex("0468aa7eb5d775b8ab32e53b3a2b006dc42638d4d5a06d9b50c493781e2a65f3e036cb273ea410534a6e8c946ae9ea7e10318686faeeba6850b16fdb2572ff0e88"),
    // The same point one octet short: a coordinate that is not a field element
    // wide is not a point, however well the rest of it reads.
    Core\Encoding::fromHex("0468aa7eb5d775b8ab32e53b3a2b006dc42638d4d5a06d9b50c493781e2a65f3e036cb273ea410534a6e8c946ae9ea7e10318686faeeba6850b16fdb2572ff0e"),
    // Ed25519's 32 raw octets offered as a P-256 point.
    Core\Encoding::fromHex("fcce9008a33565248561295a7ce1cccb040b6129bba8ff24744953bbc01261ac"),
    // A `SubjectPublicKeyInfo` cut off inside its algorithm identifier.
    Core\Encoding::fromHex("3059301306072a8648ce3d020106082a8648ce3d"),
    // A well-formed JWK of another key type.
    "{\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"x\":\"_M6QCKM1ZSSFYSlafOHMywQLYSm7qP8kdElTu8ASYaw\"}" as bytes,
    // A 1024-bit RSA key: a real key, and under the floor the roster sets.
    Core\Encoding::fromHex(""),
];
array<Core\Crypto\KeyKind> $unreadableKinds = [
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256,
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256,
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256,
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256,
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256,
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::RsaPkcs1,
];
array<Core\Crypto\KeyFormat> $unreadableFormats = [
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Raw,
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Raw,
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Raw,
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Spki,
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Jwk,
    Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Spki,
];
int $verdicts = 0;
string $sentence = "";
array<string> $sentences = [];
foreach ($unreadable as string $slot => bytes $octets) {
    try {
        Core\Crypto\PublicKey $notAKey = Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(
            $octets,
            $unreadableKinds[$slot],
            $unreadableFormats[$slot]
        );
        echo "accepted ", $slot, "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $verdict) {
        $verdicts += 1;
        $sentence = $verdict->message;
        $sentences[$verdict->message] = "";
    }
}
echo "verdicts ", $verdicts, " of ", Core\Arr::count($unreadable), "\n";
echo "distinct sentences ", Core\Arr::count($sentences), "\n";
echo $sentence, "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(): this call names an encoding the kind does not have, or hands a private key over as a public one — `Raw` is not a form either RSA kind has, and a JWK carrying `d` is the private half.
Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(): this call names an encoding the kind does not have, or hands a private key over as a public one — `Raw` is not a form either RSA kind has, and a JWK carrying `d` is the private half.
Core\Crypto\PublicKey::write(): an RSA key has no `Raw` form, so there is nothing to write — `Spki` and `Jwk` are the encodings it has.
a 2048-bit modulus is inside the roster
verdicts 6 of 6
distinct sentences 1
Core\Crypto\PublicKey::read(): these octets are not a public key of the kind and encoding this call named.
